/* I2C

I2C – Inter Integrated Circuit communication
   - Also called Two-Wire Interface

   I2C uses two signals: SCL and SDA

   SCL : Serial Clock
        Used to keep master and slave in sync during data transfer.

   SDA : Serial Data
        Line through which actual data is sent/received.

   Operating Modes
        - Master Transmitter
        - Master Receiver
        - Slave Transmitter
        - Slave Receiver
   ***************************************************************************************************
   - Every communication is started and controlled by the Master.
   - Each transfer contains an address part and a data part.
   - Data is put on SDA when SCL is LOW, and it is read when SCL becomes HIGH.
   ***************************************************************************************************

   ***************************************************************************************************
   START and STOP Conditions
   - All communications begin with START and end with STOP.
   - If SDA goes HIGH → LOW while SCL is HIGH, it means START.
   - If SDA goes LOW → HIGH while SCL is HIGH, it means STOP.
   - Only the Master can create START and STOP.
   - After STOP, the bus becomes free after some delay.
   - If a repeated START happens instead of STOP, the bus remains busy.
   - Every data unit sent on SDA is 8 bits.

   BYTE Format
   - There is no fixed limit on how many bytes can be sent in one transfer.
   - After each byte, an ACK bit must follow.
   - Data always goes MSB first.

   ADDRESS Frame
   - Communication always starts with the address frame.
   - For 7-bit addressing, the address is sent MSB first, then the R/W bit.
   - R/W = 1 → Read, R/W = 0 → Write.

   DATA Frame
   - After address frame, the data frame starts.
   - The master keeps generating clock pulses.
   - Data on SDA will be controlled either by master or slave based on R/W bit.

   Clock Speed
   - This defines how fast I2C runs and must match I2C standards.

        Standard Mode      : 100 KHz max
        Fast Mode          : 400 KHz max
        Fast Mode Plus     : 1 MHz
        High Speed Mode    : 3.4 MHz

   DUTY CYCLE
        - Ratio between SCL low time and high time.
        - Available options:
              I2C_DUTYCYCLE_2      → 2:1
              I2C_DUTYCYCLE_16_9   → 16:9
        - Selecting duty cycle helps in adjusting clock timing for required speed.
*/

#include "I2C.h"

int main(void)
{
    char rx_data[4]; // Array used to hold data read back from the slave device

    I2C1_Init(); // Setting up the I2C1 peripheral (GPIO, clock, timing, etc.)

    // Small delay just to give the slave device time to power up and get ready
    for (volatile int i = 0; i < 100000; i++);

    // Test write: sending the characters "HEY" to slave with address 0x42
    char tx_data[] = { 'E', 'S', 'C' };
    I2C1_BurstWrite(0x42, 0x00, 3, tx_data);

    // Test read: trying to receive 4 bytes from the same slave
    I2C1_BurstRead(0x42, 0x00, 4, rx_data);

    // At this point, the received data is inside rx_data[]
    // You can check it in debugger or print it through UART

    while (1)
    {
        // Staying here forever (main loop does nothing else)
    }
}
